    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "[auto-routed: 代码实验走真实执行] Implement a 1-D NLSE solver (split-step Fourier) and a collective-coordinate (CC) phase tracker. (1) Verify the solver against the exact sech soliton: L2 error at t=10 < 1e-6, energy drift < 1e-8, momentum drift < 1e-7. (2) Inject controlled roundoff by truncating the wavefunction mantissa to b bits (b = 16, 24, 32, 53) each time step, and record the CC phase φ(t) over long times. (3) Test the Wiener hypothesis: compute the phase increment variance Var[φ(t+τ)−φ(t)] vs τ and check linear scaling (diffusion constant D), plus Gaussianity of increments (QQ plot / Jarque–Bera). (4) Report D vs 2^{−2b} to confirm roundoff-driven diffusion."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The verified solver will give L2 error ~1e-9–1e-10 at t=10 with Δt ≤ 1e-3 and energy/momentum drifts matching the target tolerances. Under bit-truncated roundoff, Var[Δφ] will grow linearly in τ with slope D ∝ 2^{−2b}, and phase increments will be Gaussian — confirming the Wiener-process hypothesis. At full double precision (b=53) the diffusion constant will be too small to observe on accessible simulation times, which explains why the effect only appears under controlled precision degradation."
    },
